- F-375 **Walk's load check reads a generic declaration beside a plain one over an object type by the Return Type Rule, and its check of value types passes a type parameter.** A plain declaration whose domain lies inside an own generic's declared domain is read on the declared domains even where the lists differ only at object types, which the team's shortcut takes to exclude: the pair loads where the plain result lies below the generic's by one of three readings that the report gives, and is refused, in the rule's name, where none of them holds (`OverloadedFunction.returnRuleAtObjectArguments`; row 998, fixed). The reading at object arguments compares a tuple result element by element, as tuple types are covariant (row 1058, fixed). A declaration that writes no return type is not judged there (row 1044), a `comprises` clause is not read (row 550's pair at `ZZ64`, fixed since `ZZ64` is an object, F-379), and two plain lists keep the team's shortcut (row 697). `FTraitOrObject.enforceValueness` refuses a declared type that is not a value type below a value type, and passes the symbolic type of a type parameter (row 997, fixed). The coordinator chose to read no `comprises` clause and to leave unjudged a declaration that writes no return type; the batch's planner chose the check of value types and the repair of both directions in one step, and a worker the three readings; the curator has decided none of them. With the five number types merged with their objects (P-178, F-379), every generic-beside-plain pair over them is read on its declared domains: walk's suite loads every pair that it loaded before, and none of its expected-failure tests moves between a refusal at load and a failure at run time. The reports run the pairs found to change, old against new. Tests: `ProjectFortress/tests/GenericBesidePlainObjectReturnRuleWalk.fss`, `GenericBesidePlainObjectValidWalk.fss`, `GenericBesidePlainEveryInstanceWalk.fss`, `TraitGenericMethodValueBoundWalk.fss`, `OverloadedGenericValueBoundWalk.fss`, `NonValueBelowValueTypeWalk.fss`, `GenericBesidePlainTupleReturnWalk.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-value-bound/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why" and "Programs run old against new"; `compile-ladder/rung-number-objects/REPORT.md`, "Every pair that walk refuses at load after the change and loaded before" and "Programs run old against new"; `compile-ladder/rung-walk-load-check-generic-numeral/REPORT.md`, "Row 1058: a tuple result at object arguments (fork L2)".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-walk-load-check-generic-numeral/REPORT.md, "Row 1058: a tuple result at object arguments (fork L2)"; commit: 1caac80b3; note: the pass on the gather's rewrite at climb batch 35b's landing: the tuple clause, which read as a fourth reading inside the list of three, made its own sentence on the reading at object arguments (`belowAtObjectArguments`); the sentence on row 1122 cut, since row 1122 is two own generics, which F-275 owns, and F-257 names it among the valid pairs that walk refuses; meaning unchanged

- F-403 **The one library declares `EQV` (`≡`) and `NEQV` (`≢`) over two booleans, as top-level operators beside `<->`; walk and the compiled path under the one library run them, and on a program's own objects each is a type error.** `EQV`'s body is `if a then b else NOT b end`, which calls no `=` (compiled under the one library, `=` of two booleans dies at its first call, row 994), and `NEQV`'s is `NOT (a EQV b)`, the specification's definition of `≢` (`Specification/basic-lib/Fortress.NegatedOperators.tex`). The specification and the compiler's prelude write both as methods of `Boolean`; the one library keeps its own form, top-level operators, for the boolean operators, within P-37; that form is a default of the batch's plan, which the curator has not decided. Under walk `a ≡ b` for two objects of a program's type stops at the call, 'Unification error: Closure/Constructor for EQV param 1 (a:Boolean) got arg Pt of type Pt', and compiled it is refused, 'Could not check call to operator EQV'. Under walk, a program that imports the APL experiments' `AplCore` gets its infix `≡` and prefix `≢` over `RR64` and arrays in one overload set with the library's two: its own calls give the values that they gave without the library's two, and `t ≡ f` and `t ≢ f` of two booleans print `false` and `true`. No gated program and no line of microGPT imports `AplCore`. That one overload set is also a default of the batch's plan, which the curator has not decided. Under walk an equivalence operator that nothing declares, as `APPROX`, still loads and stops at its call with an internal error (row 1118); the one library declares none of `Boolean`'s `⊕`, `⊼` and `⊽`, and no library `majority` (row 1121). Tests: `tests/BooleanEquivalenceWalk.fss`, `one_library_tests/OneLibraryBooleanEquivalence.test`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-boolean-equivalence/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why" and "Row 184's own program, and the other programs".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-boolean-equivalence/REPORT.md, "Row 184's own program, and the other programs"; from: reviews/batch-35b-review.md, "2. The decisions that landed at a default"; commit: 1caac80b3; note: the pass on the gather's draft at climb batch 35b's landing: "fork E1 of climb batch 35" (a handle, and the batch is 35b) replaced by what the form is, fork E1's planner's default, which the curator's go of 08:4x UTC on 2026-11-23 took without deciding it; E.reserved.1's default (`PLAN.md:806`), not consequential, recorded here by the review queue's rule (his choice of 11:2x UTC on 2026-11-23): AplCore's values on the base and now '1.0', '1.0', '0.0', where `t ≡ f` and `t ≢ f` stopped on the base, 'Failed to find any matching overload, args = (true,false)'; AplCore's seven infix `≡` and four prefix `≢` at `explorations/apl/base/AplCore.fss:207-224`, `:226-228`, `:1265`; the library's line numbers (`FortressLibrary.fsi:2793-2794`, `.fss:4923-4924`) and the specification's (`:34-35`) left to the report; the AplCore program ran under walk only (`rung-boolean-equivalence/SKEPTIC.md:31`); row 994's subject stated from its claim

- F-406 **A compiled setter's call passes its value at the field's type, and a compiled call of several declarations names an overload method that the class holds.** The code generator passes the value of `c.t := v`, which the desugarer makes a call with no arrow (the checker's type for a call), at the setter's parameter type at the receiver's instance (`CodeGen.setterParamType`), also on a receiver typed by a type variable whose bounds hold the setter, cast below self's trait where it has self's type (F-377). It writes the overload method that a call names where the checker leaves it several declarations, its set split with its principal member (`CodeGen.for_RewriteFnOverloadDecl`), and, where the call's arrow is not the principal member's, a method at the call's signature that passes its arguments on (`CodeGen.forwardToPrincipal`). The written method is a default of the batch's plan, and the forwarder a worker's choice beyond the plan; the other way was to make each call through the trait. The curator has decided neither. So `c.t := High`, `s.r := self` at a type that a `comprises` clause lists, `num(R)` beside a trait's `num(self)`, a call on `self` in such a trait, and a call that one declaration takes without coercion and another by a coercion run compiled; the one library's `FortressLibrary` holds the overload methods `numerator{...}` and `denominator{...}`. The checker still refuses a comprised object's functional method at a narrower return type (row 1127). Tests: `compiler_tests/VarFieldAssignedSubtypeRun`, `FunctionalMethodComprisedObject`, `ComprisesSelfAbstractFunctionalMethodCall`, `CoercionAnyOverloadRungC`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-compiled-missing-methods/REPORT.md`, "1. What changed and why" and "5. Programs run on the old code and on the new".
  from: reviews/batch-35b-review.md, "2. The decisions that landed at a default"; from: reviews/batch-35b-review.md, "For the curator"; commit: 1caac80b3; note: the pass on the gather's draft at climb batch 35b's landing: the sentence on how the fact came about moved here: before rung M the set was split without its subsets (`os.split(false)`, no principal member, since the team's `2fb7488717` of 2012-05-09) and no such method was written in any component; fork M2's default (the written method; the forwarder the worker's device beyond its words) stated as not decided by the curator, whose go of 08:4x UTC on 2026-11-23 took it, with its other way; the arrow, the listed type and the overload methods' names glossed from the report's section

- F-253 **Under walk, a call of a generic method that writes no static argument infers the method's own static arguments from the arguments' run-time types.** This holds for a dotted call, as for a call of a generic function, whether the method is alone under its name or among its receiver's overloads. Among the receiver's overloads, where one of two is a generic dotted method and exactly one of their declared domains lies inside the other, walk chooses the inner one, except for two generic dotted methods of one name, one number of parameters and static parameters of the same kinds that differ only in their bounds, of which the first met is kept (row 1125, F-405); otherwise it compares them on the instance that the call makes (F-252). A generic method that a trait declares reads its bounds, and the types to which its arguments convert, in the trait's method environment, where its instances bind and evaluate their types, as a method that an object declares does. So it takes the instance that the same method declared in an object takes: a `ZZ32` and a `ZZ64` give `Box[\ZZ64\]` with the `ZZ32` converted, and a bounded type parameter that nothing fixes takes its bound. A generic functional method that an object inherits from a trait is chosen beside the object's plain one where only it applies. Where the inference with conversion finds no instance, as for an argument outside a bound, walk falls back to unification. For a trait's method, the unification still reads the trait's member environment, which has no parent, as the team's code did. So it does not read the bound, and the call fails with 'Cannot unify ZZ32 ... with Shape ... abm=K=(ZZ32,Any)'; the same method declared in an object fails with 'Meet(ZZ32, Shape) not a singleton'. This inference lets the team's generic `distribute` over `K` run nested reductions under walk (F-248). Gated by `tests/MethodStaticArgInference.fss`, `MethodStaticArgOverloadedReceiver.fss`, `MethodStaticArgDeclaredDomain.fss`, `NestedReductionFusion.fss`, `TraitGenericMethodInference.fss` and `InheritedGenericFunctionalMethodBesidePlain.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-method-sargs/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 2; `compile-ladder/rung-walk-trait-method-bounds/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 4, and its `SKEPTIC.md` section 3.
  from: reviews/batch-35b-review.md, "3. gather.1: a loud failure turned into a quiet wrong value"; from: reviews/batch-35b-review.md, "Corrections owed in the record"; commit: 1caac80b3; note: made false for one shape by rung L of climb batch 35b (`5c31f2b68a`): `tk[\U extends ZZ32\](y: U)` lies inside `tk[\U extends Number\](y: U)`, yet `Nested.tk(n)` answers 2, the first met kept (`XXXGenericMethodBoundsDifferWalk`); the exception named, the rest unchanged

- F-26 **Under walk, when one of two applicable declarations has static parameters of its own, the call chooses on their declared domains where exactly one lies inside the other, not by the order in which they are written.** It builds P-45, the choice among overloads by declared parameter types, under walk. A declaration has static parameters of its own if it is a generic function or constructor, or a functional method with static parameters in a trait or object without any. Its declared domain, walk's symbolic instance, is its parameter types with each type parameter standing for itself under its bounds. Where neither declared domain lies inside the other, where each lies inside the other, or where neither declaration has static parameters of its own, walk compares the domains of the instances that the call makes. So a functional method of a generic trait or object is compared on its instance, and a generic dotted method is chosen by F-253 (a generic method at a dotted call, also chosen on declared domains), except two that differ only in their bounds, of which the first met is kept (row 1125). If the chosen declaration is generic and its instance takes an argument only by a conversion, walk converts the argument and dispatches the converted arguments again, so the most specific declaration for the converted values runs. In two cases walk keeps the chosen instance, with no second dispatch: an overloaded dotted method, and a position whose conversion `Coercions.coercionFor` does not find, such as a tuple converted element by element. Two generic overloads whose static parameters agree in name, kind and bounds share one symbolic instance, even if their `where` clauses differ; no test or library declaration has such a pair (row 478). Gated by `tests/DispatchDeclaredDomain.fss` and `DispatchConvertedAgain.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-dispatch/REPORT.md` sections 5.1, 5.2 (the second dispatch) and 5.5 (the symbolic instance).
  from: reviews/batch-35b-review.md, "Findings for the record, with no decision of his changed"; commit: 1caac80b3; note: short since rung L of climb batch 35b (`5c31f2b68a`) for row 1125's shape; the exception added at the pointer to F-253, where the dotted case is given; the headline stays, its subject being the choice where both declarations reach the call

- P-174 **A numeral's type exists only at compile time: the Working Draft's `NaturalNumeral` or, with a radix point, `RadixPointNumeral`, below `Numeral`, with its four numbers as static parameters; every number type, the library's too, takes a numeral through a coercion generic over the four numbers.** The four numbers are the digit count `n`, the digits after the radix point `m`, the radix `r` and the digits read as one integer in that radix `v` (`Specification-1.0-frozen/basic/expressions/literals.tex:83-129`), so `3.125` is a `RadixPointNumeral[\4, 3, 10, 3125\]`; the four are naturals of any size; this implementation's `NN32` bound (P-40) is for sizes. A coercion's body is a computation at compile time, with no method invocation: a number used as a value there converts where it stands, as a numeral does (P-173), into the type that its place fixes, and is refused statically where it does not fit, so `value object Byte(b: ZZ32)` declares `coerce[\nat n, nat r, nat v\](x: NaturalNumeral[\n, r, v\]) where { v < 256 } = Byte(v)`; where nothing fixes a type, it is an exact integer, `ZZ`. The coercion's body calls the type's constructor on values computed from the four numbers, as in `coerce[\nat n, nat m, nat r, nat v\](x: RadixPointNumeral[\n, m, r, v\]) = Interval(roundDown(v, r, m), roundUp(v, r, m))`, and the compiler instantiates it for the numeral and folds it (P-176). No numeral value exists when a program runs: a numeral type has no getters, no `asFloat`, no `asString` and no `=` with a number type, and is written only as a coercion's or an operator's parameter type. A numeral has no sign: at a program's type `-3` is the type's own prefix `-` applied to the coercion's result for `3`, and a type without `-` refuses it; any other expression of numerals alone folds first (P-173) and reaches such a type only through its coercion from the folded value's type, such as `QQ` for `1/2`, since coercions do not chain. The design leaves out the binary chapter's numeral coercion and `opr ||` (`Specification-1.0-frozen/advanced-lib/binary.tex:1620-1662`). Why: the Draft gives numerals types of their own so that "library designers [can] decide how numerals should interact with other types of objects by defining coercion operations", and has libraries define the coercions into integers and rationals (`literals.tex:132-148`); the curator finishes that design, in place of the implementations' `IntLiteral` (P-76) and `FloatLiteral` (P-172). Row 1124 holds where the tree departs: walk loading a numeral type written as a return type, a variable's or a field's type, which it refuses only where a numeral meets it. The tree departs too, with no row, where the numeral coercions of the one library and of many tests call a getter on the four numbers, as `v.asZZ32`, and where the checker and the literals chapter read each of the four as a `ZZ` value wherever it stands (F-342, F-345).
